<figure class="flex flex-col gap-4 border-2 border-ink p-4 sm:p-6">
  <p class="font-bold">When you decide, the predictor has already filled or emptied Box B.</p>
  <div class="grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2">
    <div class="border-2 border-ink">
      <p class="border-b-2 border-ink px-3 py-2 font-bold">Already in B: $1,000,000</p>
      <dl class="text-base">
        <div class="flex justify-between gap-3 border-b border-ink px-3 py-2">
          <dt>Take B only</dt>
          <dd>$1,000,000</dd>
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-between gap-3 px-3 py-2">
          <dt>Take A and B</dt>
          <dd>$1,001,000</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>
    <div class="border-2 border-ink">
      <p class="border-b-2 border-ink px-3 py-2 font-bold">Already in B: empty</p>
      <dl class="text-base">
        <div class="flex justify-between gap-3 border-b border-ink px-3 py-2">
          <dt>Take B only</dt>
          <dd>$0</dd>
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-between gap-3 px-3 py-2">
          <dt>Take A and B</dt>
          <dd>$1,000</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-3 text-base">
    <div class="border-l-4 border-[#387a91] pl-3">
      <strong>Take both</strong>
      <p>Whatever is in B, the extra open box adds $1,000.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="border-l-4 border-[#bd4b37] pl-3">
      <strong>Take only B</strong>
      <p>
        The highly accurate prediction means this choice is usually linked to $1,000,000 in B.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <figcaption class="text-base leading-normal">
    Box A contains $1,000. The predictor put the million in B if it expected you to take B alone
    and left B empty if it expected both boxes. The numbers in each row are certain once B is
    filled; the dispute is how to choose when your action strongly predicts which row you are in
    but cannot now change its contents.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
